Z||000016||The Fieromaster||10-04-2001||02:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Predator:<BR><B>A more realistic number is I am running a pretty much stock 2.8 with a turbo setup. I run between 9 and 10 lbs of boost. I am running on the Dyno 178hp 217 Ftlbs torque. I was running a little rich though. Friday afternoon I am going on the Dyno again with a new chip so I will post if it helps the hp or not. I can't see 235 hp from a stock 2.8 with a turbo unless you are really boosting it. Maybe at the crank, but not at the wheels. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Man i have seen that much power... There has to be somthing wrong man.... Your losin power or somthings not tweaked right!  You have boost gauges and controllers right?<P><FONT SIZE="3"><B><FONT COLOR="purple"><sub>FIERO</sub><sup>MASTER</sup></FONT></B></FONT><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||1FST2M6||10-04-2001||03:46 PM||||fieromaster... it's not nearly as simple as bolt up and make boost... fuel delivery and ignition timing, the engine managements systems ability to understand the 3 bar map and it's ability to utilize what it's fed.  and uum  3.8 to 60 and 12.2 in the 1/4.. is driver abliity and available traction. un less its and AWD automatic.. don't count on being able to shift a muncie or getrag that fast. and i can vouch for the autos.. yeah tey hook up better but not that much better... you'll need a decent cam and MUCH better valve train to take the added strain of the boost and combution pressures on them.. the valve stems can bend the push rods can fail aswell...  the valve train is what we figured would have failed already on my car.. but it seem to be happy along witht he bottom end. (knock on wood) somthing else you might want to look into is methanol injection of water methanol for combustion cooling. and the obvious -- intercooling.<P>donald- when i get back from MN, we'll hook up and see aobut  your intercooler... the denser charge should lean you out a bit...     <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Phantom Rage||10-04-2001||04:26 PM||||Quote (GTMike):  “Does any one know what the HP gain per 1 psi of boost is?”<P>It really depends on your setup, but overall I have found it to be somewhere around 7.5hp/lbs of boost.  This is given that the turbo is matched correctly to the motor, for street applications.  There are many exceptions to the rule, but overall I have found this generality to be closely within ballpark range.    <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000019||GTMike||10-04-2001||05:26 PM||||thanks for the numbers guys.  7.5 hp per pound of boost.   that kinda sounds low.  i would only be geting and extra 45 hp with a 6 psi set up.   <P>the reason i want to keep it under 7psi.  is because its easer to build that set up.   i have read lots of places that you can use a riseing rate fule pressure regulator to manage the fule deliver  up to 7 psi.<P>and you can rely on the msd boost timming management system to retard the timming spacific amounts per amount of boost.  <P>i believe the only tricky part about doing a turbo is tooning it.  making sure the riseing rate fpr.  is adding the right amount of fule as the boost builds up.  and making sure that the msd unit is keeping detonation under controle.<P>sorry this is so long but  i have one more question.  since 6 pis is only suposed to get me 45 hp.  is it possible to use a 50 shot of NOS with the turbo?  keep inmind the only up grade to internals would be forged pistons and a cam or 1.6 rockers. <P>  <P>------------------<BR>87GT mostly stock, cold air intake, no cat.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_Front_Rightsmall.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by GTMike (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Capt Fiero||10-04-2001||07:32 PM||||Yes and NO!!.  Yes you can run a Turbo/Nitrous Motor but not with some serious internal mods.  When you boost a motor that 50hp shot becomes like a 150hp shot.  It supercools the turbo (assuming you put it before the turbo) it super cools the incoming aircharge that is already compressed allowing that air to be compressed even tighter, then as that super compressed air, begins to release the 50hp NOS shot, you get whamo big time power.  Some of the guys running talon turbos use a 25hp or NOS shot right off of the line to eliminate turbo lag and get the car to pull like a mad banchee, (imagine the nos as an intercooler in a bottle)  If you are staying with a stock motor, and try to add NOS and 6-8lbs of boost, you will boost your heads and maybe crank and pistons into orbit very quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000021||rogergarrison||10-04-2001||09:50 PM||||I got way over 200 with IRM and lots of engine interals. Out of the car when it was new 3 years ago, it dynoed near 275. Depends a lot on what other stuff you do. Porsche, back in 60's had a 3.0 litre I believe, that put out almost 1200 hp (not typo- twelve hundred).Ferrari GTO with 2.8 Litre was 400 hp stock trim.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||GTMike||10-04-2001||10:05 PM||||hummm maybe a 50 shot is overdoing it.  what i'm trying to do is come up with a combo that will get me around 250 at the wheels.<P>what about the nos after the turbo?<BR>or what about only haveing a 25 shot before the turbo.<P>i like the idea of intercooler in a bottle. with only a 25 shot the bottle would last alot longer too.<P>thanks for the info<P>------------------<BR>87GT mostly stock, cold air intake, no cat. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gtmike1_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||baldlobo||10-05-2001||01:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I got way over 200 with IRM and lots of engine interals. Out of the car when it was new 3 years ago, it dynoed near 275. Depends a lot on what other stuff you do. Porsche, back in 60's had a 3.0 litre I believe, that put out almost 1200 hp (not typo- twelve hundred).Ferrari GTO with 2.8 Litre was 400 hp stock trim.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>the 1200hp porsche is a turbocharged flat-10,<BR>but back in the 60's they did produce a race version of 911(R) that produced 220hp with a 2.0L engine (that had mechanical injection), now boy, don't you think a 2.8L engine should produce more than an engine that is 0.8L less than the 2.8L.<P>PS. GM doesn't know how to produce any good engines(they fall to far from the tree to hit anything european)||||reg||1
Z||000024||Neal||10-05-2001||02:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTMike:<BR><B>does any one know what the HP gain per 1 psi of boost is</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Think of it as adding more displacement not HP.  15psi = 2x displacement 3.4L becomes 6.8L.  Therefore on a 3.4L 1pst is .23L 7psi would be like adding 1.61L making your effective displacement 5.01L<P>So in very basic terms you would have the power making potential of a 5L engine  BUT that will only be under "perfect" conditions.  Turbos heat the air charge, thus making your engine less efficient, ie. less than 5L capacity.  Enters the intercooler trying to get that air charge as close to ambient temperature as possible, effectivly increasing efficiency, ie closer to 5L.<P>Turbo lag is also part of everything, if you were to drag race a natural asperated against the EXACT same car making the SAME peak HP and TQ with a smaller turboed engine the natural asperated car should win the race, because of turbo lag.<P>Good luck, turbos are FUN, very complex but FUN to play with.  And as a quick guess with a purpose built 3.4 turbo, intercooler and being PROPERLY tuned and modded, all running at 7psi  I would guess that 300hp at the crank would be obtainable.||||reg||1
Z||000025||GTMike||10-05-2001||08:14 AM||||thanks neal i like how you translated it in to displacement.  i figure cause a large displacement engien moves a certain amout of air and gass you figured out how much air and gas a turbo moves and compared the two.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||DreXteR||10-05-2001||03:34 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Capt Fiero:<BR><B>When you boost a motor that 50hp shot becomes like a 150hp shot.  It supercools the turbo (assuming you put it before the turbo) it super cools the incoming aircharge that is already compressed allowing that air to be compressed even tighter, then as that super compressed air, begins to release the 50hp NOS shot, you get whamo big time power.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I dont think if you place the NOS BEFORE the turbo will make the effect you describe. This is my theory:<P>The intercooler is to cool the air charge, it have to be @least ambient temp if isnt higher. The turbo will be generating heat also.  Lets say... NOS activates, it colds the air coming from the filter. But the turbo heat it, higer  the temp. If the charge is lower ambient temp then pass trough the intercooler that WILL heat the charge too! Why simple is as cold as ambient temp. The air charge will be getting hotter, then pass trough all the intake manifold and keep heating it.<P>The best place is after the intercooler(for sure after the BOV).<P><BR><B>The Fieromaster -</B> I havent recive the email yet. Please try agian.<P>THanx<P>DreX<P><p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Wannabe||10-05-2001||09:35 PM||||Porsche's 1200hp monster was somwhere over 5L -  I don't recall the exact figure - and was a flat 12. Highest operating  hp/L with a turbo was BMW's F1 4 cyl 1.5L turbo with somewhere over 1250hp. So the story goes, 1250hp was the upper limit of the dyno and so they never really new how much horsepower the enging could put out. This was back in '85. imagine if they had never banned turbos in F1 - how much power might they be able put out by now??||||reg||1
Z||000028||Phil||10-05-2001||11:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTMike:<BR><B>hummm maybe a 50 shot is overdoing it.  what i'm trying to do is come up with a combo that will get me around 250 at the wheels.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>To get 250 at the wheels is going to require about 310 at the crank- that's quite a bit<P><p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||1
